            <ArticleTitle>Kellgren &amp; Lawrence grade 1 osteophytes in the knee--doubtful or definite?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>149-50</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The most universally accepted methods of classification of radiographic osteoarthritis (OA) is the Kellgren &amp; Lawrence (K&amp;L) grading system. However, there have been many criticisms of this including the grading of a doubtful osteophyte as a grade 1. We report the natural history of a given prevalent grade 1 osteophyte in a 10-year follow-up knee study. 62% of women graded at baseline with a doubtful osteophyte went on to develop true osteophytic knee OA compared with only 22% of controls with no sign of disease. So called 'doubtful' osteophytes appear to be 'real' and significantly related to OA knee and therefore cannot be ignored or classified as normal.</AbstractText>
